---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import LeadStrand from '../components/LeadStrand.astro';
import LiveFrame from '../components/LiveFrame.astro';
import FeedbackTable from '../components/FeedbackTable.astro';
import Compare from '../components/Compare.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';

const V5 = 'https://metin-oasis-v6.fly.dev/';
const V6 = 'https://metin-oasis-v7.fly.dev/';
const R = '/work/review';
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="Forty-eight notes, and what happened to them"
  metaTitle="The review loop — feedback against a running build | Tyler Young"
  description="A stakeholder reviewed a running build and wrote forty-eight notes. Both versions are still deployed, so you can open the before, read the notes, and open the after."
  descriptionDraft
  eyebrow="03 · How I Work"
  meta={[
    ['Before', `<a href="${V5}">metin-oasis-v6.fly.dev</a>`],
    ['After', `<a href="${V6}">metin-oasis-v7.fly.dev</a>`],
    ['Round', '48 notes · 6 surfaces · one sitting'],
    ['Response', '4 files · 394 lines'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="answer">
    <Draft>
      Both versions are still running. Open the one that was reviewed, read what the
      stakeholder wrote about it, then open what came back. The whole exchange is
      still on the internet, which is only possible because the review happened
      against software rather than a picture of it.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>What they were given</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Not a prototype and not a deck — a deployed URL. The stakeholder opened it on
      their own machine, in their own browser, on their own time, and used it the way
      they would use anything else.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <LiveFrame
    src={V5}
    host="metin-oasis-v6.fly.dev — the build under review"
    title="Version five of the operations surface, the build that was reviewed"
    note="Running now. This is the version the notes below were written against."
  />

  <h2>What came back</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Forty-eight notes, organised by surface, written in the register of somebody
      using software rather than evaluating a proposal. Specific enough to act on
      without a follow-up meeting: rename this, colour-code that, make the first
      three columns sticky when the table scrolls sideways.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <FeedbackTable />

  <h2>What I did with them</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      The notes went straight into Claude Code against the same repository the build
      came from. That is the part that makes this loop fast: the feedback is already
      written in the vocabulary of the interface, and the agent is working inside a
      codebase governed by a design system, so “colour-code good and bad differently”
      resolves to a token rather than to an invention.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      My job in that pass was not typing. It was deciding which of the forty-eight
      were worth a version, reading what came back against the design system, and
      refusing the ones that would have cost more than they returned.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Needs>
      Tyler — how this actually ran, in your words. How long the turnaround was, and
      whether the notes went in as a batch or one surface at a time. I have not
      described the mechanics because I do not know them.
    </Needs>
  </p>

  <h2>Three notes, traced</h2>

  <Compare
    note="Progress visuals: Fix color coding — exceeding target vs falling behind should use different colors"
    before={`${R}/kpis-v5.png`}
    after={`${R}/kpis-v6.png`}
    beforeAlt="Four performance cards in a row, all with plain white backgrounds and identical amber value text, so a card 20 percent over target looks the same as one 7 percent short of it."
    afterAlt="The same four cards, each now carrying a coloured left edge with matching value text — orange, red, teal and amber — so over-target and short-of-target read differently at a glance."
    tell="Status moved from a uniform amber into four states, carried by a coloured edge as well as by the text."
  />

  <Compare
    note="Rename “Executive Intervention Queue” to “Intervention Queue”"
    before={`${R}/anchors-v5.png`}
    after={`${R}/anchors-v6.png`}
    beforeAlt="Anchor bar reading Key Performance Indicators and Intervention Que, with Queue misspelled."
    afterAlt="The same anchor bar, now reading Key Performance Indicators and Intervention Queue."
    tell="The rename also caught a spelling nobody had filed — “Que” had been sitting in the anchor bar the whole time."
  />

  <Compare
    note="Make intervention rows collapsible. Add filters (by status) and expand/collapse all icons"
    before={`${R}/queue-v5.png`}
    after={`${R}/queue-v6.png`}
    beforeAlt="Intervention section heading with a single Filter link floated to its right."
    afterAlt="Intervention Queue heading with a Filter button beneath it and a right-aligned toolbar of three controls for filtering, export and sorting."
    tell="One filter link became a control group. The collapsible rows in the same note did not land — which is the point of the table above."
  />

  <h2>What they got back</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      The next version, at its own URL, ready for the next round. Open both and the
      differences are visible without being told where to look — which is the test a
      revision should pass.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <LiveFrame
    src={V6}
    host="metin-oasis-v7.fly.dev — the next version"
    title="Version six of the operations surface, the version that came back"
    note="Running now. Thirty-six of the forty-eight notes landed here."
  />

  <p>
    <Draft>
      Twelve notes were not actioned, and that is not a failed round. A review
      produces a queue, not a checklist to be emptied. What is unusual is that the
      decision is legible at all — review a mockup and the prioritisation lives in
      somebody's memory. Review running code and it becomes a record you can audit
      later, which is exactly what I did to build this page.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <LeadStrand
    slot="after"
    items={[
      {
        slug: 'how-i-shaped-cross-functional-reputation',
        note: 'Putting a stakeholder in front of running code changes what the rest of the org thinks design is for. It is a reputational move before it is a technical one, and it only works if design has already earned the room.',
      },
      {
        slug: 'how-i-built-culture-around-paradox',
        note: 'Thirty-six of forty-eight is a judgement about which half mattered. Knowing which bars are load-bearing and which are vanity is the whole discipline of shipping inside a fixed window.',
      },
    ]}
  />
</DocumentLayout>

<style>
  .answer {
    font-size: 24px !important;
    line-height: 1.52 !important;
    margin-bottom: 36px !important;
  }
</style>
